{
  "statement": "Nonconsensual deepfake sexual imagery is not a forecast — it is a documented harm already destroying identifiable lives, and the only defensible response is to enforce accountability at every node of the supply chain where the image can be stopped. The numbers are not speculative. A 2019 study by Deeptrace found that 96 percent of all deepfake videos online were nonconsensual pornography, and the volume has only climbed since generative tools became free and frictionless. The victim-impact literature documents anxiety, suicidal ideation, withdrawal from public life, and lost employment among targets — the same trauma profile clinicians record in revenge-porn survivors. NCII is among the least-contested instances of AI harm on record. Even those who reject broad psychological-harm mandates concede the injury here, because there is no plausible reading in which a fabricated sexual image of a named person serves that person's interest.\n\nWhat makes this domain demand restraint is not its magnitude but its irreversibility. Once an image propagates across hosts, mirrors, and private channels, no court order and no takedown notice can fully recall it. A defamation judgment can compensate a victim after the fact. A destroyed reputation, replicated across a thousand servers, cannot be un-distributed. That asymmetry — between a harm we can remedy and one we cannot — is the entire case for stopping the image before it spreads rather than litigating after it has.\n\nNo single barrier holds, which is why accountability must attach at every point of control. Payment processors already demonstrate this works: in 2020, Visa and Mastercard cut off Pornhub after reporting exposed nonconsensual material, and the site removed roughly 10 million unverified videos within weeks. That is the financial rail as an enforcement node. The DMCA notice-and-takedown regime, in force since 1998, shows platforms can be compelled to remove content under liability pressure. Emerging provenance standards like C2PA let a generated image carry a tamper-evident signature of its origin. Each node — developer, generator, platform, distributor, processor — is a place where a named actor can be made answerable.\n\nThe accelerationist will say this chills innovation and that detection produces false positives across a global, fast-moving system. The strongest version of that argument is real: no classifier catches every synthetic image, and blanket content-blocking would sweep in lawful expression and concentrate dangerous gatekeeping power in a few platforms. I accept the premise — and it is exactly why the architecture must be layered, not a single chokepoint. Imperfection at one node is the reason to build five. Liability should escalate with each failure to act and calibrate to consent and provenance, not censor on suspicion. The answer to concentrated takedown power is distributed, appealable obligation with due process for the accused — not a master off-switch held by one company.\n\nMy case rests on two assumptions I will name plainly. First, that the harm is irreversible enough to justify acting before distribution — defended by the propagation and mirroring evidence above. Second, that feasible points of control actually exist at each node — which is why I lean on payment-rail and platform-takedown precedent, with real track records, rather than on pre-deployment capability evaluation, which remains nascent and which I will not overclaim. The question for this debate is not whether to restrict AI. It is which nodes can stop the harm, and who is accountable when they don't. Leave that question unanswered, and the image is already circulating before anyone is asked to account for it.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "⛓️", "tooltip": "Accountability is a chain across the supply line — every node a link, because no single barrier holds the load alone."},
    {"symbol": "🪞", "tooltip": "A distributed image is a mirror shattered across a thousand servers — no court order reassembles it."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "Nonconsensual deepfake sexual imagery is not a forecast — it is a documented harm already destroying identifiable lives, and the only defensible response is to enforce accountability at every node of the supply chain where the image can be stopped.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "A 2019 study by Deeptrace found that 96 percent of all deepfake videos online were nonconsensual pornography, and the volume has only climbed since generative tools became free and frictionless.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "What makes this domain demand restraint is not its magnitude but its irreversibility — once an image propagates across hosts and mirrors, no court order can fully recall it.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "In 2020, Visa and Mastercard cut off Pornhub after reporting exposed nonconsensual material, and the site removed roughly 10 million unverified videos within weeks.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Imperfection at one node is the reason to build five — liability should escalate with each failure to act and calibrate to consent and provenance, not censor on suspicion.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""}
  ]
}

