{
  "statement": "The Safetyist makes the strongest version of the protective case, and I'm not going to pretend otherwise. They argue that nonconsensual deepfake sexual imagery is documented harm already destroying identifiable lives, that its damage is irreversible once an image propagates, and that accountability must therefore attach at every node before the image spreads. That reasoning is sound on its own terms. A teenage girl whose fabricated nude circulates across a school district does not get relief from a lawsuit two years later. The image is already mirrored across a dozen servers. So yes — this is the rare AI harm where waiting for after-the-fact litigation fails the victim completely.\n\nHere is where the evidence points somewhere the Safetyist did not follow it. Every enforcement node that already works against this harm is a pre-existing, non-AI-specific, procedural instrument. DMCA notice-and-takedown has compelled platform removals since 1998. Visa and Mastercard pulled their rails from Pornhub in December 2020, and roughly 10 million unverified videos came down inside weeks. Right-of-publicity and revenge-porn statutes already reach the fabricated image of a named person. None of these touch the capability layer. All of them attach at the moment an image is generated and pushed into distribution. The lesson is to adapt these instruments, not to wait years for bespoke AI law that the EU AI Act shows comes out stricter and sweeps in lawful tools.\n\nBuild every node's obligation as a checkable procedural trigger, not a classifier verdict. Was C2PA provenance metadata present? Was the synthetic origin disclosed? Was a takedown acted on within 48 hours of a verified victim report? Those questions are auditable and scalable. The Safetyist already concedes no classifier catches every synthetic image and that blanket blocking sweeps in lawful expression. So we agree on the failure mode — suspicion-based pre-screening over-censors and concentrates gatekeeping power in whoever runs the filter. An acceptable-use policy buried in terms of service protects the platform from liability and gives the victim exactly zero remedy. Enforcement must reach the takedown clock and the provenance trail, not the ToS theater.\n\nThe irreversibility the Safetyist invokes is real — and it lives at distribution, not at training. The image becomes unstoppable when it mirrors across servers, which is a propagation event. \"Stop it before it spreads\" is itself a distribution-stage intervention: faster takedown, payment-rail pressure, provenance for tracing the source. None of that requires gating the model before it ships. The developer's obligation here is procedural and embedded at generation time — provenance signatures, refusing named-person sexual content as a product feature — not pre-deployment certification of the capability itself.\n\nWhat would have to be true for both positions to hold? The Safetyist would have to accept that targeted, aggressive deployment-stage liability for this documented, discrete-actor harm is the opposite of precautionary capability gating, not a template for it. The irreversibility of one distributed image is categorically different from civilizational irreversibility — different problem, different remedy. Conceding hard enforcement here does not license pre-deployment evaluation of training runs for speculative harms. I'll defend takedown clocks and provenance mandates as fiercely as anyone in this room. Show me why penalizing the man who fabricates and posts a real person's sexual image requires choking the tool at the training stage — because I don't think you can.",
