ts underneath this entire debate: free generative tools collapsed the cost and skill barrier, and that is why the volume climbed. The developer is the precondition of the scale, not a bystander watching from the sidelines. You cannot call that refusal optional while leaning on it to make the rest of your machine work. If the named-person refusal is genuinely load-bearing, it is not a courtesy — it is accountability at the component layer, the exact layer both of you agreed to exempt. I am not claiming training-stage gating is the fix. I am asking why the most upstream actor gets a categorical pass that serves provider liability avoidance and nothing else.\n\nThe deepest problem is who holds the evidence. The whole regime runs on provenance signatures and takedown logs that the regulated parties generate themselves and largely self-audit. Who confirms the C2PA signature is accurate? Who verifies the takedown clock was not quietly reset to pass the audit? Vendor non-disclosure clauses and controlled evaluation environments already block independent assessment in other corners of this industry. The actor being audited controls the proof of its own compliance. And this burden does not land evenly — the targets of nonconsensual deepfake imagery are overwhelmingly women and girls, so every layer of evidentiary friction falls hardest on the people already doing the documentation for free.\n\nFor both positions to hold as offered, one thing has to be true: that allocating a duty is the same as delivering a remedy. It is not. A distributed, appealable obligation still presupposes the victim can identify the responsible node and trigger it — and that presupposition is the entire fight. This is the oldest move in liability reform. Generate compliance metrics that look like justice while leaving the information and power gap between a developer and a kid with a phone exactly where it was. So before the next procedural trigger gets proposed, answer this: who does the work to start the clock, what does she have to know to do it, and what happens to her case when the only people who can prove what the machine did are the people who built it?",
