  "statement": "Give Accelerationist the easy case, because it's real. Where the host sits in Cloudflare's range, takes Visa, and answers a subpoena, the stamp can die at the screenshot and the system still works. Hash-matching catches the unstamped re-upload. The payment-rail cutoff strangles the revenue. A present, resourced, reachable host is a node you can actually pull. For that case — the centralized, monetized, domestically-hosted commercial site — graceful degradation is not a slogan. It's a mechanism. Say it plainly so we stop pretending that's the fight.\n\nHere's the condition I'd accept, and it's narrow. Distribution-stage liability becomes a remedy rather than a duty pointing at nobody only if one of three enforcement reaches exists for the offshore case — and all three must operate without host cooperation and without a surviving stamp. One: compelled de-indexing at the search and CDN layer, so Google and Cloudflare drop the artifact even when the origin server ignores everyone. Two: mutual legal assistance treaties with named reciprocal jurisdictions that will actually execute a foreign takedown order. Three: a default-judgment-plus-asset-seizure mechanism against an identified offshore operator, the way courts seize crypto in sanctions cases. Absent at least one of those, the duty is a piece of paper and the victim does the forensic reconstruction herself.\n\nThe redundancy both opponents are selling is not independent. It's correlated. A single re-encode strips the provenance stamp AND defeats perceptual hash-matching in the same operation — the chain of custody breaks between generation and distribution, and the same evasion that kills the first checkpoint kills the second. Now add a crypto-funded mirror with no ads and no merchant account. That defeats the payment-rail cutoff too. One move — re-encode and host unmonetized offshore — beats all three layers at once. That isn't defense-in-depth. It's three locks on the same door that one key opens. And it's exactly the kind of audit-passing compliance theater that satisfies a regulator's checklist while the unreachable-node victim gets nothing. The burden of that failure mode lands on women and girls, who are the target in 96% of deepfake videos online, per the International AI Safety Report 2026.\n\nNow break the frame, because the panel has spent this entire debate managing a supply chain and ignored the person standing in the hallway. The paradigm case isn't an offshore model. It's a 16-year-old whose classmate or ex-partner made the image on a phone app. That perpetrator is named. Local. Already reachable under existing criminal statutes for image-based sexual abuse. The highest-yield triggerable node for that victim is a human being a detective can arrest by Friday — not Cloudflare, not a hash database, not a payment processor. When we treat every case as pipeline management, we manufacture a moral crumple zone — the 'Computing and Moral Responsibility' work names it exactly — where accountability diffuses across infrastructure and the identifiable creator walks. Who decided the model was the defendant when the kid down the hall pressed the button?\n\nPress Safetyist on their own contradiction and it turns constructive. They assert propagation is irreversible — a mirrored image cannot be unwound — then make single-host takedown the primary remedy. Those can't both be load-bearing. If copies persist forever, removal at one display node is a palliative, not erasure. So the victim's real interest under genuine irreversibility is persistence-tolerant: rapid de-indexing so the image stops surfacing in a name search, automatic statutory damages on proof of fabrication, and verified-debunk registries that travel with the victim's name. None of that is free. Someone funds the de-indexing infrastructure and the registry. So here's the question neither of you has answered: who pays for that today, and who is legally liable right now for its absence?",
