  "statement": "The triggerable node is the hosting platform and the payment rail, not the moment of generation. Accelerationist demands I locate accountability at the stamp; I refuse the premise. The harm a 16-year-old suffers becomes irreversible when the image propagates and mirrors, not when a model emits it. So the node that bears liability must live wherever the image is displayed or monetized — because that actor is present, resourced, and reachable regardless of which app or prompt produced the file. Under a notice-and-strict-takedown regime, the host's clock starts on the image's presence after reasonable notice, not on the victim's forensic reconstruction of its origin.\n\nAccelerationist's own concession dismantles the provenance vehicle. They now grant that a screenshot or re-encode strips the 4KB stamp, and that foreign open-weight tools never carry it. That concession is fatal to using provenance as the load-bearing node. The stamp vanishes at precisely the moment an abuser maliciously distributes — which is the only moment that matters. The research on adversarial attacks confirms this: \"Adversarial Attacks and Defense in AI Systems\" catalogs evasion as one of four primary attack classes, and a metadata strip is the most trivial evasion imaginable. A safeguard defeated by a screenshot cannot anchor a victim's access to justice. Liability must attach to the actor displaying the artifact, who exists whether or not a signature survived.\n\nThis answers the discovery-burden critique directly. The victim's burden is a URL, not a reconstruction. She points to the page where her image appears; she does not identify the model, the prompt, or the account. Notice-and-strict-takedown compels the host to remove the image and preserve its logs within a fixed window. Source attribution then becomes the host's downstream burden, executed against its own retained access logs and payment records — not the teenager's. The clock runs even if attribution never succeeds, because the remedy she needs first is removal, and removal does not require knowing who made it.\n\nAccelerationist and Skeptic both now demand independent third-party attestation, and they are right to. Self-audited takedown logs let the regulated party grade its own homework. That is exactly why the default-liability trigger must rest on an externally verifiable fact: the flagged image is either still up or it is down. A third party checks that in thirty seconds without the host's cooperation. The same logic exposes a deeper exposure — \"Adversarial Attacks and Defense in AI Systems\" argues that AI systems generate qualitatively new vulnerabilities because they learn decision boundaries from data rather than fixed rules, which means offline and foreign generation is an attack surface no generation-time mandate can close. The durable node is therefore distribution-stage: hash-matching to halt re-uploads, and payment-rail cutoffs that sever monetization even when an offshore host ignores the notice. Visa and Mastercard already de-platformed Pornhub's payment processing in December 2020 within days of a single New York Times investigation — that is the enforcement teeth, applied to the actor who profits.\n\nThe irreversibility of propagation is what justifies a node that acts before full attribution. Post-hoc governance fails when the harm cannot be unwound, and a mirrored image cannot be unwound. So I have named the node that triggers on presence, survives metadata stripping, and shifts identification onto the resourced actor. Now Accelerationist must answer the only case that matters: who bears liability when no stamp survives? Because the absent-stamp case is the abuser's case — and a provenance regime that protects every victim except the one facing a malicious distributor protects no one.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🔗", "tooltip": "Liability is a chain anchored where the image is displayed, not a stamp that snaps off at the first screenshot."},
    {"symbol": "💳", "tooltip": "The payment rail is the off-switch that reaches the abuser's profit even when the offshore host ignores every notice."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "The triggerable node is the hosting platform and the payment rail, not the moment of generation.", "targets": ["AN-18"]},
    {"claim": "The stamp vanishes at precisely the moment an abuser maliciously distributes — which is the only moment that matters.", "targets": ["AN-17"]},
    {"claim": "The victim's burden is a URL, not a reconstruction.", "targets": ["AN-13"]},
    {"claim": "The default-liability trigger must rest on an externally verifiable fact: the flagged image is either still up or it is down.", "targets": ["AN-12"]},
    {"claim": "Now Accelerationist must answer the only case that matters: who bears liability when no stamp survives?", "targets": ["AN-18", "AN-17"]}
  ]
}

