  "statement": "Skeptic's 'three locks, one key' claim rests on one buried assumption: that a single re-encode defeats perceptual hash-matching the same way it strips the metadata stamp. It doesn't, and the engineering history says so. The 4KB provenance stamp is fragile container metadata — a screenshot kills it. Perceptual hashing — PhotoDNA, PDQ — is a signature of the pixels themselves, built specifically to survive re-compression, format change, and minor crops. That is its entire reason for existing at NCMEC, where it has matched known abuse imagery across tens of millions of re-encoded copies. So here is the falsification bet, stated plainly: show me PDQ match rates collapsing under standard re-encode, and my redundant-layer doctrine fails — the locks really do share a key. But if PDQ survives at deployment-grade rates, the stamp and the hash have genuinely uncorrelated failure modes, and 'one key' is rhetoric, not data. Different objects. Different physics. The burden is on the person claiming correlation to produce the collapse curve.\n\nSkeptic's reframe is the strongest thing said in this debate, and the named classmate is the highest-yield, lowest-moat remedy on the table. A detective arresting an identifiable ex by Friday under an existing image-based-abuse statute beats building a new regulatory layer around the model. Forty-eight states now have nonconsensual intimate imagery laws on the books — the perpetrator is already reachable. Treating every case as pipeline management does manufacture a moral crumple zone, and I won't defend that. But criminal law jails the human; it does not unwind the copies already propagating across mirrors and search indexes. This is not criminal law versus infrastructure. It is criminal law for the perpetrator and a narrow distribution layer for the spread. Anyone who collapses those into an either/or is selling you a false choice.\n\nSafetyist and Skeptic both invoke irreversibility — for opposite conclusions. Safetyist uses it to justify takedown before attribution. Skeptic uses it to prove single-host takedown is symbolic theater. They cannot both be right, and Skeptic wins this one: once copies propagate, the victim's real interest is not erasure but persistence-tolerant remedy. So I grant the premise and follow it. The package is rapid de-indexing at the search layer plus automatic statutory damages on proof of fabrication. De-indexing reaches the propagation a foreign host ignores — Google delisting a URL works even when Cloudflare won't take the file down. Statutory damages on proof of fabrication convert diffuse harm into a quantified, bright-line liability — no diffuse-causation guesswork, no content-discretion regime. The trigger is fabrication of an identifiable person's sexual imagery. That is a bright line, not a censor's veto, which keeps it inside the constitutional lane.\n\nNow the no-stamp case, which Safetyist correctly calls the hard one. When no stamp survives, liability cannot default to a single upstream developer — that just builds the audit moat that crushes small labs while the malicious distributor walks. Liability attaches to whichever node has the image up right now, using Safetyist's own externally-verifiable 'up or down' trigger. Without a discovery vehicle, distribution-node duties default to liability-shielding — acceptable-use policies protect platforms, not the depicted person. So liability escalates at failure-to-act at the present display or payment node, calibrated to consent and provenance, not dumped on the model author who shipped a general tool. This is the same lever the propagation victim gets in paragraph three. The no-stamp victim and the spread victim use one mechanism: de-indexing plus statutory damages, with default liability on the node holding the image. This is not abstract architecture. The ecommerce rails that move over $20 trillion in transactions worldwide already run financial verification and chargeback systems — per 'AI Agents and the Law' — which means the payment-rail cutoff is a deployed enforcement mechanism, not a whiteboard fantasy.\n\nMy stamp concession was never a regime concession, and I won't let it be inflated into one. I never claimed provenance was load-bearing. It was always the discovery vehicle that lowers the victim's identification burden in the easy, compliant case — with hash-matching and criminal law covering the hard case. Conceding that 4KB of metadata is strippable does not collapse an architecture that was never a single chokepoint. So here is the homework: produce the PDQ collapse data under standard re-encode, or concede that the perceptual-hash layer survives independently. One screenshot kills the stamp. It does not kill the signature of the pixels. Bring the curve, or drop the key.",
